WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.122
A reranker adds a second stage to retrieval.

00:00:03.122 --> 00:00:10.932
The first-stage retriever is fast and brings back a candidate set, perhaps the top ten or top twenty chunks.

00:00:10.932 --> 00:00:15.812
Then a cross-encoder looks at the question and each candidate together.

00:00:15.812 --> 00:00:22.493
Because it evaluates the pair directly, it can often produce a better ranking than vector similarity alone.

00:00:22.977 --> 00:00:24.694
The tradeoff is speed.

00:00:24.694 --> 00:00:32.794
Rerankers are usually more expensive than the first-stage search, so we do not run them across the entire corpus.

00:00:32.794 --> 00:00:38.334
The pattern is retrieve broadly, then rerank narrowly.

00:00:38.334 --> 00:00:45.514
Use reranking when the correct source is often retrieved but appears too low in the candidate list.

00:00:45.514 --> 00:00:49.719
Again, evaluation should drive the decision.

00:00:49.719 --> 00:00:57.189
If the baseline already places the correct chunk at rank one, adding a reranker may increase latency without improving

00:00:57.189 --> 00:00:58.926
the user experience.
